  "party_positions": [
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "Petitioner (Malcolm L. Stewart)",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0000-0001"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 3,
                "start": 3
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Petitioner (Malcolm L. Stewart), according to Petitioner (Malcolm L. Stewart), section\u202f52(a)\u2019s disparagement provision is a reasonable limit on a government program, not a speech restriction, and therefore does not violate the First Amendment."
        }
      ],
      "heading": "Petitioner (Director, USPTO) Position",
      "status": "complete",
      "summary": null,
      "summary_citations": []
    },
    {
      "claims": [
        {
          "attribution": "John C. Connell",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0001-0013"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 30,
                "start": 30
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to John C. Connell, the registration denial blocks the applicant from obtaining trademark protection, creating a burden that harms the ability to compete in the marketplace."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0003"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 39,
                "start": 39
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Connell, the register simply records marks that the government has approved, similar to other ministerial registrations such as patents or vehicle titles."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0004"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 44,
                "start": 44
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Connell, viewpoint discrimination is prohibited in commercial speech under the Sorrell case."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0006"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 45,
                "start": 45
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Connell, disparaging messages in trademarks control a component of the overall message but do not interfere with source identification."
        },
        {
          "attribution": "Mr. Connell",
          "citations": [
            {
              "document_hash": "ce7908ff3073fe9be72d42d42f015739b01abb257c801b122bade49fca10ede5",
              "evidence_ids": [
                "ev-ce7908ff3073-0002-0007"
              ],
              "pages": {
                "end": 42,
                "start": 42
              }
            }
          ],
          "text": "According to Mr. Connell, all distinctions made by the Lanham Act are viewpoint\u2011neutral and further its commercial objectives of reducing consumer confusion."
        }
      ],
